Adults who engage in learning activities have a 30–40% lower risk of Alzheimer's disease, according to longitudinal studies — cognitive stimulation is a proven neurological protective factor.
The concept of neuroplasticity, established by Marian Diamond and later Michael Merzenich, shows the adult brain physically changes with learning — 'use it or lose it' is neurologically literal.
Viktor Frankl's logotherapy research showed that meaning-making activities (which include learning and mastery) are among the strongest predictors of psychological resilience and wellbeing.
